    "result_text": "I implemented `answer()` in `app/chat.py` and added four tests to `tests/test_chat.py`. `pytest -q` passes (6 tests).\n\n**What it does**\n- Each attempt runs `iter_answer` from the start and joins the text. Nothing is kept from an attempt that failed, so the result only ever holds the text of the attempt that finished.\n- A failure to connect, or a connection that drops mid-answer, logs a WARNING on `app.chat` (for example `Attempt 2/3 failed: RemoteProtocolError: peer closed connection without sending complete message body`) and retries. After `ATTEMPTS` failures it raises `UpstreamUnavailable`, with the last error attached as its cause.\n- A timeout raises `UpstreamTimeout` straight away, with no retry.\n- The client is used as given, and nothing new was installed.\n\n**Two things about openai 3.19.2 that shaped this** (I checked the installed package source):\n1. **The network error's message isn't in the SDK's error.** The library now wraps a dropped stream too, not just a failed connection. Either way you get an `APIConnectionError` whose message is always just \"Connection error.\" The real network error, such as \"peer closed connection\u2026\", is only kept as the exception's cause, so the log line reads it from there. Logging the SDK exception's own text would have given ops nothing to tell the failures apart.\n2. **A timeout is a kind of connection error.** `APITimeoutError` is a subclass of `APIConnectionError`, so the timeout case is handled first. Otherwise timeouts would be retried like any other connection error.\n\n**One small extra:** `history` is copied into a list first. A one-shot iterator (like a generator) would otherwise be used up by the first attempt, and the retries would go out without the earlier conversation.\n\n**Tests:** they raise the errors the same way the SDK does, using real `httpx2` errors as the cause. They cover:\n- a normal answer\n- a connection failure, then a dropped stream, then success: only the final text is returned, and both warnings include "
  },
